{"kind":"request","text":"Bound commit messages in the gate (item 5 of request ea7e2aa6).\n\nKernel promise 1 says that the record is the interface, and the Git log is part of the record. Since 1 October, 856 of the 3,911 subjects on main are longer than 100 characters, and the coherence review counted 22 with words glued to hashes, for example commit 13193f31: \"exactb8a4dea1/tree73587f1b\".\n\nThe bound:\n1. A subject has at most 100 characters. Detail goes in the body, after one blank line.\n2. No word is glued to a hash: no run of three or more letters directly before or after a hexadecimal run of seven or more characters that holds at least one digit. The check reads the subject and the body. Trailer lines at the end (Name: value, such as Gitseq-Approval) are not checked.\n3. The check covers each commit that the head adds over its base, the same base that gate.sh uses for whitespace. History on main does not change.\n\nAdd a gate step, commit-messages, to scripts/gate.sh, with its script and Node tests. The tests cover the example above, subjects of 100 and 101 characters, and tokens such as base64url, sha256 and ed25519 that the check must accept. Run the check once over the subjects on main since 1 October and report what it flags; it changes none of them. Update docs/testing.md.\n\nLanding texts given to gs merge follow the same bound from now on: a first line of at most 100 characters, then the detail. The checker's landing audit reports a breach.\n\nOrder: after 7b19837c lands. It touches no package code, so it can go beside other work.","body":{"conditions":"One head: scripts/gate.sh, the new check script and its Node tests, and docs/testing.md. One gate at the head; checker review at the exact head before landing.","priority":"after 7b19837c lands; beside other work","target":"inherit","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}